## 4. MARGIN & LEVERAGE FRAMEWORK

### 4.1 When to Use Leverage

Kelly formula produces $f^* > 1$ when $\mu > r + \sigma^2$.

This mathematically signals that borrowing to invest is optimal.

### 4.2 Hard Safety Caps

| Parameter | Cap | Reasoning |
|-----------|-----|-----------|
| Maximum leverage | **2.0×** (50% margin) | 1987-style 23% crash at 2× = survivable 2.85× ratio |
| Theoretical Kelly leverage | Ignore if >2.0 | Continuous models don't account for gap risk |
| Margin call buffer | Never exceed Reg T initial margin | Margin calls force liquidation at worst possible time |

### 4.3 Same-Day Substitution Rule (Reg T §220.3(g))

**Mechanism:** Liquidate existing hedge → released capital applied to new hedge of equal value on the same day → no new cash required.

**When no immediate trade available:** Park released buying power in short-term listed bonds. Preserves ability to deploy when the next anomaly appears.

